    {
      "page_title": "Indexing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 1,
          "title": "Indexing",
          "tagline": "find rows without scanning"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "a separate sorted structure, usually a ==B-tree==, mapping column values to row locations"
            },
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "no index means a full table scan, **O(n)**; a B-tree lookup is **O(log n)**"
            },
            {
              "icon": "table",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "WHERE filters, JOIN keys, ORDER BY, and enforcing UNIQUE constraints"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. How it works",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- users: 10,000,000 rows",
            "SELECT * FROM users",
            "WHERE email='a@x.com';",
            "-- no index: scans every row",
            "CREATE INDEX idx_users_email",
            "  ON users(email);"
          ],
          "note": "same query, now ~4 B-tree levels",
          "result": {
            "headers": [
              "Plan",
              "Time"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Full scan",
                "~2 s"
              ],
              [
                "Index, 4 levels",
                "< 1 ms"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. What you gain",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Index type",
            "Why it helps"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Single column",
              "reads go **O(n)** to **O(log n)**"
            ],
            [
              "Composite (country, created_at)",
              "`WHERE country='IN' ORDER BY created_at`, no sort"
            ],
            [
              "Covering",
              "holds every selected column, index-only scan"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Watch out",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE also updates each index: **writes slow down**",
            "Leftmost-prefix rule: index (a,b) helps `WHERE a=1`, not `WHERE b=2`",
            "`WHERE LOWER(email)=...` wraps the column and skips the index",
            "Low-cardinality columns, like a boolean, gain little"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY:",
          "text": "Index for your read patterns, state the write cost, and prove it with `EXPLAIN`."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "think B-tree!",
      "subtitle": "Skip the full scan"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Transactions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 3,
          "title": "Transactions",
          "tagline": "BEGIN ... COMMIT"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "a group of statements that ==all take effect== (COMMIT) or **none** do (ROLLBACK)."
            },
            {
              "icon": "shield-check",
              "label": "Why:",
              "text": "a crash halfway through a multi-step change would leave data inconsistent."
            },
            {
              "icon": "table",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "money transfers, order plus stock decrement, any change across rows or tables."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. How it works",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "UPDATE accounts SET bal=bal-100",
            "  WHERE id=1;",
            "UPDATE accounts SET bal=bal+100",
            "  WHERE id=2;",
            "COMMIT;"
          ],
          "note": "autocommit needs an explicit BEGIN",
          "result": {
            "headers": [
              "id",
              "bal before",
              "bal after"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "1",
                "500",
                "400"
              ],
              [
                "2",
                "200",
                "300"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "If the second UPDATE fails, run `ROLLBACK`.",
            "Account 1 returns to its original **500**; no money is lost.",
            "Others never see a half-applied state."
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Benefits vs. pitfalls",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Topic",
            "Point",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "{green:Benefit}",
              "All-or-nothing",
              "No partial state"
            ],
            [
              "{green:Benefit}",
              "Savepoints",
              "Undo part, keep rest"
            ],
            [
              "{red:Consider}",
              "Long transactions",
              "Locks block, undo bloats"
            ],
            [
              "{red:Consider}",
              "Autocommit",
              "Each statement commits alone"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "none"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "INSERT INTO orders VALUES (9);",
            "SAVEPOINT s1;",
            "UPDATE stock SET qty=qty-1;",
            "ROLLBACK TO s1; -- keeps the order",
            "COMMIT;"
          ],
          "note": "ROLLBACK TO undoes only later work"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Common mistakes",
          "ink": "red",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Holding a transaction open while waiting on a user.",
            "Calling HTTP or email inside it; rollback cannot **unsend** them.",
            "Assuming two statements are atomic under autocommit."
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY:",
          "text": "Keep transactions short, scope each to one logical operation, and name exactly what must be atomic."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "lock",
      "aside": "one unit of work",
      "subtitle": "All or nothing"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "ACID",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 3,
          "title": "ACID",
          "tagline": "predictable storage"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "four guarantees a transaction system gives: ==Atomicity==, Consistency, Isolation, Durability"
            },
            {
              "icon": "shield-check",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "makes concurrent, crash-prone storage behave **predictably** for app code"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "choosing relational (ACID by default) vs NoSQL stores that relax it"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Four letters, four mechanisms",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Letter",
            "Guarantee",
            "Mechanism"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**A**tomicity",
              "all or nothing",
              "undo log"
            ],
            [
              "**C**onsistency",
              "rules hold",
              "constraints"
            ],
            [
              "**I**solation",
              "no half-done view",
              "isolation level"
            ],
            [
              "**D**urability",
              "survives crash",
              "WAL + fsync"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. How each one works",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Atomicity**: a failed transaction is undone from the undo log",
            "**Consistency**: foreign keys and `CHECK balance>=0` hold before and after commit",
            "**Isolation**: concurrent transactions never see each other's half-done work",
            "**Durability**: the ==WAL== is fsynced at commit, so committed data survives a crash"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Example: a bad transfer",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- A=40, B=500, CHECK(bal>=0)",
            "BEGIN;",
            "UPDATE acc SET bal=bal-100",
            "  WHERE id='A';  -- 40-100 = -60",
            "COMMIT;  -- CHECK fails, ROLLBACK"
          ],
          "note": "rejected whole, never half-applied",
          "result": {
            "headers": [
              "Account",
              "Before",
              "After"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "A",
                "40",
                "40"
              ],
              [
                "B",
                "500",
                "500"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. The price of ACID",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Cost",
            "Why"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Latency",
              "WAL fsync per commit = disk wait"
            ],
            [
              "Cross-shard",
              "needs two-phase commit, much slower"
            ],
            [
              "Scope",
              "ACID holds per node only"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Common mistakes",
          "ink": "red",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Confusing ACID's C with CAP's C",
            "ACID C = your **rules** (constraints) hold",
            "CAP C = replicas **agree** on one value",
            "Assuming a cross-shard transaction is as fast as a local one"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY:",
          "text": "Name each letter with its mechanism: undo log, constraints, isolation, WAL."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "A-C-I-D, one mechanism each",
      "subtitle": "Four guarantees"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Isolation Levels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 4,
          "title": "Isolation Levels",
          "tagline": "how much T1 sees of T2"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "shield-check",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "a setting for which ==anomalies== concurrent transactions may observe"
            },
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "full isolation is slow, so you trade **correctness** for throughput"
            },
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "tuning per transaction: `SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL ...`"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The four levels",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Level",
            "Stops",
            "Note"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Read Uncommitted",
              "nothing",
              "dirty reads allowed"
            ],
            [
              "Read Committed",
              "dirty reads",
              "Postgres, Oracle default"
            ],
            [
              "Repeatable Read",
              "+ non-repeatable",
              "MySQL InnoDB default"
            ],
            [
              "Serializable",
              "+ phantoms",
              "equals a serial order"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Non-repeatable read",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- Read Committed",
            "-- T1: SELECT bal;     -- 500",
            "-- T2: UPDATE bal=300; COMMIT;",
            "-- T1: SELECT bal;     -- 300"
          ],
          "note": "same query, different answer",
          "result": {
            "headers": [
              "Step",
              "T1 sees"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "first read",
                "500"
              ],
              [
                "after T2 commit",
                "300"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Lower levels give ==higher concurrency== and fewer aborts",
            "Higher levels buy correctness with waiting and retries"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Watch out",
          "ink": "red",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Write skew**: two doctors both go off call, still possible under Repeatable Read or snapshot",
            "Serializable can **abort**, so the client must retry on a serialization failure",
            "Picking Serializable everywhere kills throughput"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY:",
          "text": "Name the anomaly you are guarding against, then choose the weakest level that prevents it."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "lock",
      "aside": "name the anomaly!",
      "subtitle": "Pick the weakest safe one"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "MVCC",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "Multi-Version Concurrency Control keeps ==many versions of a row==, so each transaction reads a consistent snapshot"
            },
            {
              "icon": "lock",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "readers never block writers, and writers never block readers"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "{icon:postgresql} PostgreSQL, {icon:mysql} MySQL InnoDB, and Oracle snapshot reads"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Row Versions",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every row version carries a creator and a deleter transaction ID",
            "PostgreSQL names them `xmin` (creator) and `xmax` (deleter)",
            "An **UPDATE** writes a new row version",
            "The old version stays until no snapshot needs it"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Worked Example",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "tables",
          "spec": {
            "tables": [
              {
                "title": "accounts, id=1",
                "headers": [
                  "version",
                  "balance",
                  "xmin",
                  "xmax"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "old",
                    "500",
                    "90",
                    "101"
                  ],
                  [
                    "new",
                    "300",
                    "101",
                    "-"
                  ]
                ],
                "caption": "T101 updates and commits"
              }
            ],
            "arrow": false
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "Txn",
            "Action",
            "Sees"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1",
              "T100",
              "BEGIN; SELECT balance",
              "500"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "T101",
              "UPDATE to 300; COMMIT",
              "-"
            ],
            [
              "3",
              "T100",
              "SELECT balance again",
              "==500=="
            ],
            [
              "4",
              "T102",
              "new txn reads balance",
              "300"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Benefit vs Price",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Benefit",
            "Price"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Plain reads take **no locks**",
              "Dead versions pile up as bloat"
            ],
            [
              "Read-heavy loads scale",
              "PostgreSQL needs `VACUUM`"
            ],
            [
              "Point-in-time reads: backups, reports",
              "Long txn pins old versions"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Thinking MVCC stops two writers on one row; they still conflict",
            "Leaving a transaction open for hours, which blocks cleanup",
            "Ignoring bloat until tables and indexes swell",
            "Assuming plain reads need locks, so avoiding replicas for reports"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY:",
          "text": "Say MVCC means readers don't block writers, then name its price: version cleanup (VACUUM)."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "snapshots, not locks!",
      "subtitle": "Readers vs writers"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Locking",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 6,
          "title": "Locking",
          "tagline": "two buyers, one last item"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "lock",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "a mechanism that makes concurrent ==transactions wait== for each other on shared data"
            },
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "two writers changing the same row would otherwise **lose** an update"
            },
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "inventory counts, account balances, and unique allocations"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Lock modes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Shared (S)== lock: many readers hold it at once",
            "==Exclusive (X)== lock: only **one** writer, nobody else",
            "A writer waits until every other lock on the row is released"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Pessimistic: lock first",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "SELECT stock FROM items",
            "WHERE id=7 FOR UPDATE;",
            "UPDATE items SET stock=9",
            "WHERE id=7;",
            "COMMIT; -- lock released"
          ],
          "note": "row stays locked until COMMIT"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Optimistic: check at write",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "UPDATE items",
            "SET stock=9, version=4",
            "WHERE id=7 AND version=3;",
            "-- 0 rows updated: retry"
          ],
          "note": "no lock held, so retry on conflict"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "",
            "Pessimistic",
            "Optimistic"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Locks",
              "row held till COMMIT",
              "none taken"
            ],
            [
              "Benefit",
              "no lost update",
              "cheap, no waiting"
            ],
            [
              "Best for",
              "high contention",
              "low contention"
            ],
            [
              "On clash",
              "others wait",
              "zero rows, retry"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Deadlock and lock traps",
          "ink": "red",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Deadlock**: T1 locks row A then B, T2 locks B then A; the DB aborts one",
            "Lock rows in a ==consistent order== in every code path",
            "Row locks are cheap, but a **table lock** blocks every other writer"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY:",
          "text": "Choose pessimistic for high contention and optimistic for low, and know how to avoid deadlocks with consistent lock order."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "lock",
      "aside": "wait your turn!",
      "subtitle": "making writers take turns"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Connection Pooling",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 8,
          "title": "Connection Pooling",
          "tagline": "borrow it, then give it back"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "a cache of open DB connections that requests ==borrow and return=="
            },
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "a new connection costs TCP + TLS + auth, often **20-50 ms**, plus a server process"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "web apps, serverless, microservices: HikariCP, PgBouncer, pgpool"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. How it works",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "App thread",
                "sub": "500 of them",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "Wait queue",
                "sub": "480 wait",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "Pool",
                "sub": "20 open",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "text": "Database",
                "sub": "sees 20",
                "ink": "red"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pool opens ==10 connections== at startup",
            "A request **borrows** one, runs its query, **returns** it",
            "500 threads share 20; the other 480 queue up"
          ],
          "style": "number",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Benefits and sizing",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Topic",
            "What happens"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Latency",
              "No per-request connect cost"
            ],
            [
              "Load cap",
              "Blocks connection storms"
            ],
            [
              "PgBouncer",
              "Thousands of clients, few server links"
            ],
            [
              "Postgres limit",
              "max_connections = 100 default"
            ],
            [
              "Pool size",
              "(cores x 2) + disks to start"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 4
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "java",
          "lines": [
            "// HikariCP config",
            "maximumPoolSize = 20;",
            "connectionTimeout = 3000;",
            "try (Connection c = ds.getConnection()) {",
            "  // run query; auto-release",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "always release in finally"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Common mistakes",
          "ink": "red",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Huge pool: each link eats memory and causes ==contention==",
            "Leaked connections never return and **exhaust** the pool",
            "No timeout, so callers hang forever on an empty pool",
            "Pool sizes added up across instances beat max_connections"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY:",
          "text": "A smaller, bounded pool beats a large one; mention PgBouncer for serverless."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "small pool, big win!",
      "subtitle": "Borrow, don't reconnect"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Partitioning",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 8,
          "title": "Partitioning",
          "tagline": "one table, many pieces"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "table",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "One large table split into smaller physical {icon:database} **partitions** inside a single database"
            },
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "Huge tables make queries, indexes, and maintenance slow"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Time-series logs, events, and orders that grow forever"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. How it works",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Range== by month: `orders_2026_01`, `orders_2026_02`, and so on",
            "==Hash== spreads rows evenly: `hash(user_id) % 8`",
            "==List== groups rows by region, like EU or US"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT * FROM orders",
            "WHERE created_at >= '2026-03-01'",
            "AND created_at < '2026-04-01';"
          ],
          "note": "reads only orders_2026_03",
          "result": {
            "headers": [
              "Partition",
              "Read?"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "orders_2026_02",
                "pruned"
              ],
              [
                "orders_2026_03",
                "**scanned**"
              ],
              [
                "orders_2026_04",
                "pruned"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The planner skips every other partition. This is ==partition pruning==.",
          "frame": "none"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Benefits",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Task",
            "One big table",
            "Partitioned"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "One month of orders",
              "scans everything",
              "reads 1 partition"
            ],
            [
              "Remove old data",
              "slow DELETE, millions of rows",
              "DROP partition, **instant**"
            ],
            [
              "Index size",
              "one huge index",
              "small, fits in memory"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Consider",
          "ink": "red",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Queries that omit the partition key scan **all** partitions",
            "Bad key makes a hot partition: monthly ranges write only the current month",
            "Partitioning stays on one machine, so it adds no capacity beyond that server"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY:",
          "text": "Partitioning is within one database. Say it before you reach for sharding."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "prune, don't scan!",
      "subtitle": "Split one big table"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Replication",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 10,
          "title": "Replication",
          "tagline": "same data, many nodes"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "keeping ==copies of the same data== on multiple database nodes"
            },
            {
              "icon": "shield-check",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "to **survive** node failure and to scale reads"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "read replicas, high availability, cross-region reads, backups"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "title": "1 primary, 3 replicas",
            "rows": [
              [
                "app"
              ],
              [
                "primary"
              ],
              [
                "r1",
                "r2",
                "r3"
              ]
            ],
            "nodes": {
              "app": {
                "text": "App",
                "shape": "icon",
                "icon": "laptop"
              },
              "primary": {
                "text": "Primary (writes)",
                "shape": "icon",
                "icon": "database",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              "r1": {
                "text": "Replica 1",
                "shape": "icon",
                "icon": "database",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              "r2": {
                "text": "Replica 2",
                "shape": "icon",
                "icon": "database",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              "r3": {
                "text": "Replica 3",
                "shape": "icon",
                "icon": "database",
                "ink": "blue"
              }
            },
            "edges": [
              {
                "from": "app",
                "to": "primary",
                "label": "writes"
              },
              {
                "from": "primary",
                "to": "r1",
                "label": "WAL"
              },
              {
                "from": "primary",
                "to": "r2",
                "label": "WAL"
              },
              {
                "from": "primary",
                "to": "r3",
                "label": "WAL"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Single-leader==: the primary takes writes, streams its WAL or binlog to followers",
            "The app sends writes to the primary and spreads `SELECT`s across the replicas",
            "Primary dies? A replica is promoted: **failover**"
          ],
          "style": "number"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Benefits vs trade-offs",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mode",
            "Write cost",
            "Catch"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Asynchronous",
              "low latency",
              "replica lag, stale reads"
            ],
            [
              "Synchronous",
              "waits for ack",
              "no loss, slower writes"
            ],
            [
              "Multi-leader / leaderless",
              "low latency",
              "write conflicts"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Read throughput scales roughly with ==replica count==",
            "Failover cuts downtime from hours to **seconds**",
            "Conflicts need a rule: last-write-wins or CRDTs"
          ],
          "style": "checkbox",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Common mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Write, then read a lagging replica: the user sees stale data",
            "Adding replicas to scale writes: every node applies every write",
            "Going multi-leader with no conflict-resolution rule",
            "Choosing synchronous everywhere and paying the ack latency"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY:",
          "text": "Replication gives read scale and availability; the price is consistency lag."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "copies, not shards!",
      "subtitle": "copies for reads and uptime"
    },
